  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round1_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B035",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for optical lattice clocks at the magic wavelength"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B041",
    "B027"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "Optical lattice clocks are an unusually clean case of a fundamental measurement idea becoming a mature physical instrument. The magic-wavelength concept resolved a central limitation of trapping atoms for optical-frequency metrology, and the subsequent experimental realization has delivered reproducible control of systematic shifts at exceptional precision. Katori and Ye provide a compact attribution spanning the decisive concept and its defining experimental establishment. The clocks are now independently realized and useful not merely for timekeeping but as sensitive probes of gravity and fundamental physics.",
    "case_against": "The prize wording must distinguish the original lattice-clock principle from the much wider optical-clock and precision-metrology enterprise, to which many groups contributed. A prize centered on an instrument also requires care that the fundamental physical advance, rather than technical performance alone, remains clear.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll regards X-ray free-electron lasers as a qualitatively new ultrafast coherent X-ray capability with unequivocal maturity across several operating facilities.",
        "response": "I agree that B041 has enabled remarkable science and remains a serious alternative. But its indispensable contributions extend across theory, accelerator development, facility construction, and large teams, making the proposed three-person attribution harder to delimit than for B035. The clock achievement has a more sharply identifiable physical principle and credit line."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
        "point": "Mehlig emphasizes that fluctuation theorems revealed a universal structure for irreversible small systems and have been tested across mesoscopic and molecular experiments.",
        "response": "This is a profound conceptual achievement, and the experimental tests make B027 stronger than a purely formal proposal. I nevertheless retain B035 above it because lattice clocks unite a specific foundational control mechanism with a broad, precise, independently reproduced experimental field; for B027 the balance among the original theorem, later formulations, and a singular experimental breakthrough needs further adjudication."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson argues that atomic quantum information processing and simulation has produced controlled, reproducible demonstrations over decades, while noting overlap among atomic platforms.",
        "response": "The maturity of B013 is compelling. Its overlap with broader trapped-ion and quantum-information developments is, however, a real attribution and scope difficulty. B035 is narrower and has a more direct relation between its core idea and the mature measurements that followed."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether the citation can isolate magic-wavelength lattice clocks without inadvertently crediting the broader optical-clock field.",
      "The historical division of credit between the original concept, early realization, and later systematic-control advances."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
